# This working tree is ~478 GB. Almost all of that is copyrighted game/firmware material and
# machine-regenerable output, and NONE of it belongs in git - see "Never commit" below.
# What we track is the ~10 MB that is actually ours: code, docs, research text, manifests.

# ── Copyrighted assets. Never commit, never push. ─────────────────────────────
# Decrypted game dumps (owned-copy dumps: legal for us to hold, not to redistribute)
/dumps/
# Preserved patch estate (EU + US chains, ~331 GB)
/psn-acquisition/pkgs-eu/
/psn-acquisition/pkgs-us/
# Jailbreak payloads, restore images, merged PKGs
/cfw-staging/
# (the destiny1/ dossier, its firmware corpus and archived talks now live under
# historicaldocs/ and external/, both ignored below)
# Belt-and-suspenders by extension, wherever they appear
*.pkg
*.PKG
*.PUP
*.dec
*.7z
*.zip
*.iso
*.img
*.mp4
*.mkv
*.mov
*.pptx
*.avi

# ── Agent/harness working state. Never publish. ───────────────────────────────
# Claude Code's per-project state. `.claude/worktrees/` alone was 1.04 GB of checked-out
# worktree - untracked but NOT ignored, so any `git add -A` would have swept it in.
# Also holds agent skills, local settings and whatever future harness state lands here.
.claude/
.codex/
.aider*
# Scratch/handoff files agents leave behind
*.orig
*.rej
*.bak-2*

# ── Regenerable / machine output ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Per-experiment run artifacts (emulator logs run to tens of MB each and grow without bound).
# Provenance lives in each run's RUN-INFO.txt on the box; promote anything worth keeping into docs.
/runs/
/captures/
/logs/
d1-server/target/
# The standalone crates (d1-observe, d1-trace) are deliberately OUTSIDE the d1-server workspace and
# carry their own target/ - an anchored `d1-server/target/` rule missed them, which is why
# `git add d1-observe d1-trace` proposed 12,257 files. Unanchored: any target/ at any depth.
target/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo

# ── Secrets. Nothing here is tracked, and nothing should ever be added. ───────
*token*
*.pem
*.key
.env
.netrc

# ── Editor / OS noise ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# ── Keep these despite the broad rules above ──────────────────────────────────
!.gitignore

# Bungie manifest archive (large, and not ours to redistribute)
manifest/

# Derived from the manifest above, so it inherits both reasons: a few MB of Bungie's item data,
# regenerable in seconds with `d1-loadout build`. The loadouts in `loadouts/` are ours and ARE
# tracked - they are the curated part.
catalogue/

# ── EBOOT- AND CONTENT-DERIVED ARTIFACTS: REGENERATE, DO NOT REDISTRIBUTE ──────
# These are mechanically extracted from the game's own eboot and content packages,
# so they are derivative works of copyrighted material - the same category as the
# decompiled corpus, which design/ghidra.md already refuses to ship ("keep how to
# install and drive Ghidra and how to reproduce our best-state decomp FROM YOUR OWN
# DISC; ship no Ghidra, no decompiled corpus, no project").
# The GENERATORS ship. The OUTPUT does not. Each regenerates in one command against
# a disc you own, with its own controls, and refuses to write if they fail.
#   capture-rig/eboot-atlas.py          -> eboot-{atlas,types,namehash,callgraph}.tsv
#   capture-rig/diagnostic-catalogue.py -> diagnostic-catalogue.tsv
#   capture-rig/live-namespace.py       -> namespace-map.{json,tsv}
capture-rig/eboot-atlas.tsv
capture-rig/eboot-callgraph.tsv
capture-rig/eboot-types.tsv
capture-rig/eboot-namehash.tsv
capture-rig/diagnostic-catalogue.tsv
capture-rig/namespace-map.json
capture-rig/namespace-map.tsv
capture-rig/namespace-tag-identity.json
capture-rig/schemas/
capture-rig/manifests/

# Third-party reference checkouts (large, not ours, not redistributable)
external/

# Historical/superseded docs: local reference only, never published
/historicaldocs/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# capture-rig derived data - untracked 2026-08-11.
# Policy: this repo ships the GENERATORS, not the extracted data. A user brings
# their own dump and regenerates everything below. On-disk copies were kept
# with `git rm --cached`; nothing was destroyed.
# ⚠ A trailing "# comment" on a pattern line does NOT work: `#` only starts a
# comment at the start of a line, so the path becomes a pattern with spaces and
# matches nothing. Comments go above their pattern, and every one is verified
# with `git check-ignore -q`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# regenerate: d1-eboot thiscalls
/capture-rig/eboot-thiscalls.tsv
# regenerate: d1-eboot imports --tsv
/capture-rig/eboot-imports.tsv
# regenerate: d1-bap message-table --tsv (values also carried as Rust consts)
/capture-rig/bap-message-table.tsv
# regenerate: d1-oracle item-index write  (byte-identical to the Python's output
# apart from the generator byline; proven by `d1-oracle item-index diff`)
/capture-rig/item-content-index.tsv
# regenerate: d1-oracle art-arrangement write
/capture-rig/item-art-arrangement.tsv
# regenerate: d1-oracle activity-modes write  (the d1-ghost destination selector data)
/capture-rig/activity-modes.tsv
# LICENSING, not convenience: 1,114 symbol names extracted from copyrighted
# packages must not ship. ⛔ NO generator exists in any language yet, so this is
# the one entry that fails the ship-the-generator test. Writing a d1-pkg
# symbol-inventory writer is required work, not optional.
/capture-rig/world-namespace.tsv
